Directional Selectivity is intentionally not a uni-
versal profitability filter. Among 212 evaluated
Stage 3 combinations, 81 were rejected because
their realized direction did not remain stable as se-
lectivity tightened. Of these rejected combinations,
47 still obtained positive after cost cumulative re-
turn in the held out evaluation. The filter therefore
discards a meaningful set of profitable but direc-
tionally unstable signals, especially rebound and
panic selling patterns. This exclusion is the cost
of restricting FaVOR to directionally consistent
signals.
